1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:05,460
Let's look together at a second 
example: the check-order one. 

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:12,540
In this case, we have only one shared variable 
called turn. Its value represents which thread  

3
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:18,060
is allowed to enter the critical section.
The entry section of each thread does a  

4
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:22,440
busywait loop until it does not 
see its id in the variable turn. 

5
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:30,300
Once it completes its critical section, each 
thread sets the value of the other thread into  

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:37,380
the variable turn. When A is done it allows B 
to enter the critical section and vice-versa. 

7
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:40,680
Is this a valid solution to 
the critical section problem?

8
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:47,940
Let's start by checking the mutual exclusion 
property, in the same case we were looking  

9
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:54,060
at before, that of A entering its critical 
section while B is in its critical section. 

10
00:00:55,260 --> 00:01:00,420
Before entering its critical section, 
A must see that turn's value is A. 

11
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:06,660
Can B see the value of turn 
being "B" during this period? 

12
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:13,260
The answer is no because A will set it 
to B only after the critical section.

13
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:21,120
Notice that if you think it could because you 
are considering the case in which the period  

14
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:28,500
in which A's critical section happens is here, 
then you are considering a portion of time during  

15
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:35,340
which the execution is valid, but the case we are 
looking at needs still to be explored assuming A  

16
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:44,400
sees turn value being A after B completes line 4.
Now that we know that A cannot enter its critical  

17
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:51,480
section while B is in it, we need to check if B 
can enter its critical section while A is in it. 

18
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:56,100
If you think about this, you can immediately 
conclude that this can be shown with the same  

19
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:59,580
example, by switching A with 
B, so the answer is still no.

20
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:06,420
Good, we have proven we cannot find an 
example that breaks mutual exclusion. 

21
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:12,780
Let's check the progress property now.
Can one of the threads be willing to enter the  

22
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:18,900
critical section but unable to do that while the 
other thread is also outside its critical section? 

23
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:25,320
For instance, let's say A is in its 
waiting loop because turn's value is B. 

24
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:31,620
Can it happen that B does not put turn 
value to A in a bounded time? Yes,  

25
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:38,040
B can be in its remainder section and, as we 
said, the duration of the reminder section  

26
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:42,120
is arbitrarily long, which means 
the progress property is violated.

27
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:50,820
We can then conclude this code is not a valid 
solution to the critical section problem,  

28
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:53,580
because it breaks the progress property.

